{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} 4QBSD Dialog}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 4QBSD_Dialog}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn\tx355 \f4\b\fs36\cf1 4 Quadrant Back Scattered Detector Dialog
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\tx355 \f13 This panel is used to control the \b 4\plain\f13\fs24  \b Q\plain\f13\fs24 uadrant \b B\plain\f13\fs24 ack \b S\plain\f13\fs24 catter \b D\plain\f13\fs24 etector (4QBSD).
\par \pard\tx355 \f4 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx8495\cellx8995\cellx13465\pard\intbl \f13 Each detector segment can be set up individually or by using one of the \uldb \b Predefined Settings\plain\f13\b\fs24 {\v 4QBSD_Predefined_Settings}\plain\f13\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl In the \b Quadrants\plain\f13\fs24  group, each quadrant can be configured. See \uldb \b BSD Bias\plain\f13\b\fs24 {\v 4QBSD_Bias}\plain\f13\fs24 .
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The \b Mode\plain\f13\fs24  group allows predefined settings for the 4 segments to be set up.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b Topo\plain\f13\fs24  should be used when configuring the detector for \i Topographical\plain\f13\fs24  imaging
\par\intbl \b Compo\plain\f13\fs24  should be used when configuring the detector for \i Compositional\plain\f13\fs24  (or Atomic Contrast) imaging.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The BSD Gain, Auto Range and Fast fields control aspects of the BSD signal amplifier. \cell\pard \pard\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm1.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par If the \b BSD Auto Range\plain\f13\fs24  is checked \{bmc bm2.bmp\} the Gain is automatically set by the signal contrast level.
\par For more precise setting of BSD contrast, deselect\b  BSD Auto Range\plain\f13\fs24  and select a \b Gain\plain\f13\fs24  setting suitable for the sample. Contrast adjustment range now covers the selected gain range.
\par 
\par The \b BSD Fast\plain\f13\fs24  field is manually set and should be set \{bmc bm3.bmp\}\f4  when searching for an area of interest using fast scan rates, and disabled\b  \plain\fs24 when imaging at slow scan rates and also for photo recordings.
\par \pard \f13 
\par \b Defining Topo settings
\par \plain\f13\fs24 To change the configuration set the individual segment settings as required and press \b BSD: Set TOPO\plain\f13\fs24 . 
\par These settings will be remembered and used whenever the TOPO command is given.
\par 
\par See also \uldb \b Mounting of  Back Scattered Detector\plain\f13\b\fs24 {\v Mounting_of__Back_Scattered_Detector}\plain\f13\fs24  and \uldb \b Operation Principle of Backscattered Detector\plain\f13\b\fs24 {\v Operation_Principle_of_Backscattered_Detector} \plain\f13\fs24 and\b  \uldb Parameters\plain\f13\b\fs24 {\v 4QBSD_Parameters}\plain\f13\fs24 
\par 
\par 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} 4QBSD Parameters}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} 4QBSD_Parameters}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} 4QBSD Dialog}
\f4\b\fs36\cf1 4QBSD Parameters
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par The data and comments below shall provide for optimal adjustment of all parameters.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx8495\cellx12965\pard\intbl \b Working Distance
\par\intbl \plain\fs24 
\par\intbl The detector has a maximum sensitivity for the back scattered electrons at a working distance of 6 mm if the 10 mm diode pack is fitted or a range of 12 to 20mm if the 25mm diode pack is fitted. At shorter working distance many electrons travel through the central bore  in the detector and at long working distances more and more electrons pass by the sensitive area of the detector diode. 
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl \f11 
\par\intbl \f10\fs20 \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx8495\cellx12965\pard\intbl \f4\b\fs24 Tilt Angle
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \plain\fs24 When the surface is oriented normal to the primary electron beam the signal level is the highest and it decreases as the tilt angle increases.  Therefore no strong signal can be expected from a highly tilted specimen surface because the electrons are scattered in forward direction and do not reach the detector anymore.
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm4.wmf\}
\par\intbl \cell\intbl\row
\trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx8495\cellx12965\pard\intbl \b High Voltage\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The higher the energy of the backscattered electrons the better the collection efficiency. 
\par\intbl There is a low KV limit of about 3 to 5 kV below which the detector will not detect backscattered electrons
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl \cell\intbl\row
\pard In order to obtain the best signal the highest voltage should be selected which the specimen will withstand consistent with good imaging.  Therefore the optimal setting must be considered for each application.
\par \page
