{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Absorbed Specimen Current Measurement}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Absorbed_Specimen_Current_Measurement}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Absorbed Specimen Current Measurement
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par Principle of Absorbed Specimen Current Measurement:
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx9065\cellx13815\pard\intbl\tx355 When the primary electron beam (\b PE\plain\fs24 ) hits the specimen, a number of different signals are generated from the specimen:
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b SE\plain\fs24 :\tab Secondary electrons of low energy
\par\intbl \b BSE\plain\fs24 :\tab Back scattered electrons of higher energy
\par\intbl \b TE\plain\fs24 :\tab Transmitted electrons from thin specimens
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Some electrons are absorbed in the specimen and must be conducted to ground potential. If the specimen is insulated from ground these electrons can be measured as specimen current (\b SC\plain\fs24 ) by an amplifier connected between the specimen and the chamber ground.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl An equilibrium of charge is built on the specimen giving the following relation of electron currents:
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \tab \tab \b I\plain\fs24 PE =\b  I\plain\fs24 SE +\b  I\plain\fs24 BSE + \b I\plain\fs24 TE + \b I\plain\fs24 SC
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl and thus
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \tab \tab \b I\plain\fs24 SC = \b I\plain\fs24 PE -\b  I\plain\fs24 SE -\b  I\plain\fs24 BSE - \b I\plain\fs24 TE
\par\intbl \b \cell\pard \pard\intbl\tx355 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm6.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard\tx355 
\par \page
