{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Beam Current Measurement}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Beam_Current_Measurement}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} About Box}
\b\fs36\cf1 Beam Current Measurement
\par \pard\li705\ri25 \plain\f10\fs20 
\par \pard \f4\fs24 The primary beam current can be measured with a Faraday cage.
\par 
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx7935\cellx12685\pard\intbl A hole is drilled into the base material of the receptor (1) which has the inner walls painted with carbon to obtain high absorption of electrons. The hole is covered with a diaphragm (2) which is fastened with a spring ring (3).
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl The \b primary electron beam\plain\fs24  (4) is \b centered\plain\fs24  into the bore of the diaphragm (2) allowing the electrons to enter the hole (1). There the electrons are being absorbed. Electrons which are reflected (5) cannot leave the Faraday cage and get absorbed on its walls as well. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Thus all electrons are conducted through the Specimen Current Measuring System (6) and are measured there.
\par\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm7.wmf\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par For Beam Current Measurement use the \b Faraday Cup \plain\fs24 accessory.
\par \page
