{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Operation Principle of Backscattered Detector}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Operation_Principle_of_Backscattered_Detector}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Operation Principle of Backscattered Detector
\par \pard \plain\fs24    
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx10485\cellx13535\pard\intbl While secondary electrons are most often used to display topography contrast the backscattered electrons are used to display the materials contrast because the backscatter coefficient is dependent on the mean atomic number of the material under investigation. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b Depth Information
\par\intbl \plain\fs24 Those electrons which form the back scattered image stem from deeper regions of the specimen.
\par\intbl The sketches on the right show the principle:
\par\intbl The primary electron beam (PE) penetrates under the surface of the specimen because of its high energy. There the electrons interact with the material and are scattered back to the surface (BSE). The mid grey area in the sketch on the left shows approximately the region in which the primary beam is scattered. The real size of the scattering area varies with material and high voltage and serves here as a model only. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl On the left a cut an polished specimen is sketched where a dense phase (mid gray) is embedded in a light matrix (white). On the left side the edge of the phase runs perpendicular to the specimen surface, on the right side the edge is inclined. Although the phase is under the specimen surface where the primary electron beam (PE) hits the surface back scattered electrons (BSE) travel to the surface and add to the image.
\par\intbl This defocuses the edge more the further the beam travels to the right. On the real image below this effect can be seen at the right edge of the particle very clearly.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl Unsharp edges and fuzzy particles are due to the depth effect. Also the problems generated for stereometric evaluation can be seen since the dimensions of the phase can not be identified correctly. 
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b Image:\plain\fs24 
\par\intbl Mineral particle embedded in resin, cut and polished and coated with carbon.\cell\pard \pard\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm27.wmf\}
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm28.wmf\}
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \{bmc bm29.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Registration Algorithm}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Registration_Algorithm}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Registration Algorithm
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The algorithm used is two point registration which requires the locations of 2 marks at the extremities of the specimen co-ordinate system to be defined in terms of the stage absolute co-ordinate system.
\par This algorithm assumes that the user co-ordinate system has the same units in both X and Y directions.
\par 
\par If the user co-ordinate system has different units in X and Y (e.g. rows and columns of structures) then \ul \b\ul 3 point alignment\plain\b\fs24 {\v 3_point_alignment}\plain\fs24  may be selected.
\par 
\par For a given registration the Rotation and Tilt axes are constant and the relationship between the XY  co-ordinate systems computed from the positions of the two points in each system
\par \pard 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Registration sequence}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Registration_sequence}
\pard \b\fs36\cf1 Registration sequence
\par \plain\fs24 
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 1. Choose two points (registration marks) on the sample having known positions in the sample co-ordinate system. These rwo points should be (as near as possible) at the origin and the extremity of the co-ordinate system
\par 2. At low magnification use the joystick to navigate over the specimen to ensure both registration points may be located. Adjust rotate and tilt as necessary.
\par 3.For best accuracy switch on Stage Backlash Correction
\par 4. Display the \uldb Stage Registration Dialog\plain\fs24 {\v Stage_Registration_Dialog}
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 5. Select the Stage List required (e.g. \b Reg 1\plain\fs24 )
\par 6. Enter the registration name and units.
\par 7. Position the first registration mark at the centre of the screen (Use centre point if required) increasing the magnification and centering again until a magnification is reached suitable for the conditions of inspection required. Press \b Record Stage Position : First Point. \plain\fs24 You may be asked to confirm that the Rotate and Tilt values should be updated. Reply \b Yes
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \plain\fs24 8. Enter the sample co-ordinates for this point in \b First Registration Point : Mapped Position
\par \plain\fs24 9. At low magnification use the joystick to locate the second registration mark.
\par 10. Position the second registration mark at the centre of the screen (Use centre point if required) increasing the magnification and centering again until a magnification is reached suitable for the conditions of inspection required. Press \b Record Stage Position : Second Point. \plain\fs24 If you are asked to confirm that the Rotate and Tilt values should be updated you have accidently changed the rotate or tilt, Reply \b NO \plain\fs24 and use \b Move Axis Tilt/Rotate\plain\fs24  to move the stage to the correct tilt/rotation, reposition the mark to centre and record again.
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 11. Enter the sample co-ordinates for this point in \b Second Registration Point : Mapped Position
\par \plain\fs24 12. If required confirm positioning by using\b  Go To Absolute position First Point/Second Point \plain\fs24 to confirm correct locations are recorded.
\par 13. Press \b Register\plain\fs24 
\par 14. Save registration data to file 
\par 
\par 
\par Note: If registration data is available from file and the sample position on the stage is similar the the previous occasion load the file and use the \b Move Axis Tilt/Rotate \plain\fs24 to set stage to registration values and use \b Go To Absolute position First Point/Second Point \plain\fs24 to position the stage roughly at the registration mark positions.
\par \page
