{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} specimen current Imaging}
\pard\keepn {\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} specimen_current_Imaging}
\b\fs36\cf1 specimen current Imaging
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 The Specimen Current Monitor may be used as an imaging device.
\par \b Image Mode\plain\fs24 
\par 1.\tab Select the \uldb \b Specimen Current\plain\b\fs24 {\v Specimen_Current_Monitor}\plain\fs24   panel (\b Tools - Go To Panel\plain\fs24  and select \b Specimen Current Monitor\plain\fs24 )
\par 2.\tab Obtain a secondary electron image and bring it to focus.
\par 3.\tab Select \b SCM \plain\fs24 from the \b Detection -\plain\fs24   \b Detectors \plain\fs24 menu.
\par 4.\tab Select \b SCM Image \plain\fs24 from the \b Specimen Current\plain\fs24  panel, set \b Brightness \plain\fs24 to 50% and adjust \b Contrast \plain\fs24 until an image is seen. Then adjust \b Brightness \plain\fs24 and \b Contrast \plain\fs24 to obtain the required image.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par At low probe currents i.e. on SCM ranges lower than 1nA full scale, the image can become degraded by mains field effects and other forms of interference. Also, on these ranges, the signal bandwidth is very low. The use of long scan times will overcome the bandwidth restrictions and will also help to reduce interference effects, but image quality may not be perfect under these conditions.
\par \page
